            "captions": {
                "linkedin": "How We Reclaimed Our Morning Routine (Without Throwing Phones Out the Window)\n\nThere was a point last year where my alarm went off at 6 AM and I'd already spent twenty minutes scrolling before my feet hit the floor. My coffee would get cold. My daughter, Nora - then barely walking - would be standing in her crib waving at me through the railings while I tapped through Instagram on the potty. That was not how I wanted our mornings to look.\n\nSo we changed things. Not dramatically. Not overnight. But enough that now most days actually start with us together rather than each of us locked inside our own little screen bubble.\n\nHere's what worked:\n\n1. Charge devices outside the bedroom.\nThis one comes from Dr. Matthew Walker's work at UC Berkeley on sleep and technology. Even if you're not sleeping next to your phone, having it within arm's reach changes your brain's response patterns during rest. We moved our chargers into the hallway closet. It took two days to adjust. Now? I don't wake up reaching for anything except Nora when she starts babbling.\n\n2. Replace the scroll with something worse - at first.\nI'm serious. The hardest part of breaking an old habit isn't building a new one; it's surviving the five minutes after waking up when nothing else feels satisfying. So in those empty moments, I started doing push-ups on the bathroom floor while waiting for the shower water to heat up. It sounds ridiculous. It felt terrible for the first week. By the second week, though, my body craved movement instead of mindless swiping.\n\n3. Make breakfast interactive.\nNora gets her oats with blueberries and yogurt. She drops half of them on the kitchen counter. That's fine. What matters is that she's sitting across from me, and neither of us have screens between us. I k...\n\n#DigitalGuardian #DigitalParenting #DigitalSafety #ParentingTips",
